@translation labeled en project

#lg 5.1	proverb

@(A.5.1.1 = 1-5)  @varh[t6105.v1]{The elephant spoke to himself: @q{“There is nothing like me among all the creatures of @wDN{Šakkan}!”} The wren (?) answered him: @q{“But I, in my own small way, was created just as you were!”} } @vart[t6105.v2,]{@note{1 ms. has instead:} The elephant spoke to himself: @q{“Among all the creatures of @wDN{Šakkan}, the one that can defecate like me has yet to be created!”} The wren (?) answered him: @q{“But I, in my own small way, can defecate just as much as you!”} }

#etcsl#altGrpo
#etcsl#alt t6105.v1 t6105.v2
#etcsl#altGrpc
#lg 5.2	proverb

@(A.5.2.6 = 6)  @varh[t6105.v3]{You go like an elephant to raise a sunken boat. } @vart[t6105.v4,]{@note{1 ms. has instead:} An elephant is led to a sunken boat to raise it. }

#etcsl#altGrpo
#etcsl#alt t6105.v3 t6105.v4
#etcsl#altGrpc
#lg 5.3	proverb

@(A.5.3.7 = 7)  @note{cf. @xref[t.6.1.03,t.6.1.03.14]{6.1.03.14}} The wild bull is taboo for the plough.

#lg 5.4	proverb

@(A.5.4.8 = 8)  @q{“Like the wild bull, you only do what pleases you.”}

#lg 5.5	proverb

@(A.5.5.9 = 9)  The wild bull wanders about amid the rushes.

#lg 5.6	proverb

@(A.5.6.10 = 10)  The wild bull is free from the plough (?).

#lg 5.7	proverb

@(A.5.7.11 = 11)  The wild bull …… I abandon the claim.

$ (1 ms. adds 4 proverbs:)

#lg 5.8	proverb

@(A.5.8.11A = 11A-11B)  The wild bull …… enveloped …… above …….

#lg 5.9	proverb

@(A.5.9.11C = 11C)  Wild bull, you …… your strong limbs.

#lg 5.10	proverb

@(A.5.10.11D = 11D)  The wild bull …… aggressively.

#lg 5.11	proverb

@(A.5.11.11E = 11E)  The wild bull ……. 
$ (end insertion)

#lg 5.12	proverb

@(A.5.12.12 = 12)  An ox will catch you in your path.

#lg 5.13	proverb

@(A.5.13.13 = 13)  Like an ox, you do not know how to turn back.

#lg 5.14	proverb

@(A.5.14.14 = 14)  The dog snarls at an ox scrubbed with soap.

#lg 5.15	proverb

@(A.5.15.15 = 15)  The ox has been tamed; its tamers are great.

#lg 5.16	proverb

@(A.5.16.16 = 16)  The ox …….

#lg 5.17	proverb

@(A.5.17.17 = 17)  @note{cf. @xref[t.6.1.02,t.6.1.02.86]{6.1.02.86}, @xref[t.6.1.26,t.6.1.26.d6]{6.1.26.d6}} The furrows seem pleasant to an ox on the threshing floor.

#lg 5.18	proverb

@(A.5.18.18 = 18-19)  The ox …….  @gap{1 line fragmentary}   @gap{unknown no. of lines missing} 


#lg 5.25	proverb

@(B.5.25.1 = 1-2)  The ox ……  @gap{1 line fragmentary} 

#lg 5.26	proverb

@(B.5.26.3 = 3)  From so many oxen, is there no dung?

#lg 5.27	proverb

@(B.5.27.4 = 4)  If you make the oxen multiply, you may collect their dung.

#lg 5.28	proverb

@(B.5.28.5 = 5)  O ox, you wander about in the street like a commissioner.

#lg 5.29	proverb

@(B.5.29.6 = 6)  The owner of an ox is also happy at being the emissary at the city gate.

#lg 5.30	proverb

@(B.5.30.7 = 7)  The cow jumps to her heart's content, but she does not gore.

#lg 5.31	proverb

@(B.5.31.8 = 8)  O cow, like a cat you walk behind the man carrying the food basket.

#lg 5.32	proverb

@(B.5.32.9 = 9)  No one will give away even a barren cow for nothing.

#lg 5.33	proverb

@(B.5.33.10 = 10)   @gap{1 line fragmentary} 

#lg 5.34	proverb

@(B.5.34.11 = 11)  Like a cow that has not given birth, you are looking for a calf of yours that does not exist!

#lg 5.35	proverb

@(B.5.35.12 = 12)  The cow walks in the marsh, the calf on the dry ground.

#lg 5.36	proverb

@(B.5.36.13 = 13-17)  A calf entered ……. It did not know how to get out. When the herdsman came to inspect, he hurled the churn towards it, smashing the churn. When the calf came out, it cowered -- but the churn was still smashed.

#lg 5.37	proverb

@(B.5.37.18 = 18)  Like a horse you paw the ground where you drink.

#lg 5.38	proverb

@(B.5.38.19 = 19-21)  @note{cf. @xref[t.6.2.3,t623.h34]{6.2.3: UET 6/2 287}} The horse, after throwing off his rider, said: @q{“Were my load to be like this forever, how weak I would become!”}

#lg 5.39	proverb

@(B.5.39.22 = 22-23)  @note{cf. @xref[t.6.2.3,t623.h35]{6.2.3: UET 6/2 283}} The donkey, after he had thrown off his packs, said: @q{“Now I can forget the burdens of former days!”}

#lg 5.40	proverb

